 <h2 id="strb"><code>strb</code></h2>
-<p class="mod-desc">layer: core The string toolkit operating on BYTE-PACKED strings — the representation of a literal and of set_char-built buffers. This is the ROUND-10 answer to the R9 gap: a programmer holding &quot;hello world&quot; can now use the toolkit directly, with no hand-built char-code array. THE TWO REPRESENTATIONS, side by side (kept consistent with the char-code array (lib/string.smlib R9 toolkit): qword cells, one char code per cell, read with h[i] (8-byte stride). Idiom: vitti h = nirmmita(6*8); h[0]=104; h[1]=105  (&quot;hi&quot; as cells); str_find(h, 2, nd, 1). byte string (this module): bytes packed 1-per-byte, read with char_at(s, i) (1-byte stride), written with set_char(s, i, v), measured with vartani_len(s). Idiom: strb_find(&quot;hi&quot;, 2, &quot;i&quot;, 1) -&gt; 1; or vitti b = nirmmita(8); set_char(b,0,104); set_char(b,1,105); set_char(b,2,0); strb_len(b) -&gt; 2. Do NOT mix them: h[i] on a byte string reads 8 bytes at once, and char_at on a char-code array reads one byte of a qword cell. This lives in its own module (not inside string.smlib) because the compiler has a fixed AST ceiling: inlining the combined module together with the other stdlib modules exceeded it (suite tests 87/99/102). The char-code toolkit in lib/string.smlib is byte-for-byte unchanged. Conventions: lengths are explicit (source of truth); out-buffers get a 0 terminator via set_char; the caller allocates generously. Empty needle matches at 0; &quot;not found&quot; is -1. All signatures stay within the compiler&#x27;s 6-argument cap (repl is 0-terminated, its length derived). SLICE-BOUNDARY RULES (WS4, R23 audit): a slice is a (pointer, length) pair into a larger buffer, with NO terminator at the slice end. - Every strb_* function takes an explicit length and stops at it: strb_find_char(&quot;hello world&quot;, 5, &#x27;w&#x27;) is -1. The NUL-terminated forms (find_char, count_char, count_vowels, is_all_digits, str_equals) scan to the terminator, so they are WRONG on slices. Never pass a slice to them. - strb_len / str_len / vartani_len scan to the terminator: they cannot be used on slices at all. A slice carries its length. - strb_replace / str_replace&#x27;s repl is 0-terminated (its length is derived), so repl cannot be a slice. To use a slice as repl, strb_dup it into a terminated buffer first. An explicit-length repl form would need 7 arguments, over the compiler&#x27;s 6-argument cap, so the terminated form stays. - The (s, i) char predicates take no length: the caller must keep i in bounds; they read char_at(s, i) unconditionally. strb_split/strb_join use the same length-prefixed segment format as str_split/str_join (R9): [len0, b0, b1, ..., len1, b0, ...], one byte value per cell. strb_pack(cells, n, out) — pack a qword-cell sequence (one byte value per cell, the str_split/strb_split segment format) into contiguous bytes. Writes a 0 terminator at out[n]. Returns n. This is the boundary helper: hand-written repack loops (one per call site, each a chance to get the stride wrong) become one call. n &lt;= 0 writes only the terminator and returns 0. POINTER ARITHMETIC IS IN BYTES: to pack starting at cell i of a cell array, pass cells + i*8, not cells + i.</p>
